    overview_html: `<p><strong>The Crew Motorfest</strong> centers cosmetic monetization on vehicle cosmetics and player character customization using Crew Credits premium currency. Cosmetics focus on vehicle paint jobs, body kits, and performance visual modifications that enhance the driving experience. The cosmetic shop integrates vehicle customization with cosmetic purchases, creating practical cosmetic value.</p><p>Vehicle cosmetics offer better visibility and engagement than typical cosmetics since players spend entire game sessions controlling cosmetically customized vehicles. This makes cosmetic pricing somewhat justified by visibility and engagement time.</p>`,
    pity_explanation_html: `<p>The Crew Motorfest uses a direct cosmetic shop without gacha mechanics or pity systems. Vehicle cosmetics are purchased directly at fixed prices; the cosmetic shop features no randomization or guaranteed rewards.</p>`,
    history_html: `<p><strong>2023:</strong> The Crew Motorfest launched with a cosmetic shop focused on vehicle customization and cosmetics. Initial vehicle cosmetics were priced competitively with racing game standards.</p><p><strong>2023-2024:</strong> Seasonal cosmetics expanded the vehicle customization catalog. Limited-time cosmetics began emphasizing seasonal themes and exclusive vehicle cosmetics.</p><p><strong>2024:</strong> Cosmetic bundling strategies increased, with cosmetic packs encouraging vehicle cosmetic collection. Seasonal cosmetics became more prominent monetization drivers.</p><p><strong>2024-2025:</strong> Continued vehicle cosmetic expansion with thematic seasonal cosmetics. The cosmetic pricing remained competitive with racing games but continued to emphasize seasonal limited-time availability.</p>`,
    controversy_html: `<p>The Crew Motorfest's cosmetic monetization generates mild criticism for seasonal vehicle cosmetic pricing and time-limited availability. However, the criticism is less severe than other games due to the practical cosmetic visibility during extended gameplay sessions. Some players note cosmetic prices are high for cosmetic-only modifications without gameplay functionality.</p>`,
    tips_html: `<p><strong>1. Customize your favorite vehicle:</strong> Focus cosmetics on vehicles you actually drive; cosmetic visibility matters in games where you control the cosmetic item.</p><p><strong>2. Seasonal cosmetics rotate:</strong> New vehicle cosmetics arrive seasonally; wait for seasonal rotations rather than purchasing at full price when possible.</p><p><strong>3. Bundle evaluation:</strong> Vehicle cosmetic bundles often pair cosmetics together; evaluate if you want all cosmetics in the bundle.</p><p><strong>4. Free cosmetics exist:</strong> Seasonal challenges and progression rewards provide vehicle cosmetics without credits spending; prioritize free cosmetics.</p><p><strong>5. Color customization value:</strong> Paint jobs offer the most visibility and can be applied to multiple vehicles; prioritize paint cosmetics for value.</p>`,
    editorial_html: `<p>The Crew Motorfest's cosmetic shop benefits from the racing game genre's practical cosmetic visibility. Unlike cosmetics that only other players see, vehicle cosmetics are visible to the player controlling the vehicle during entire racing sessions. This visibility justifies some cosmetic pricing that would be predatory in other genres.</p><p>The fairness score of 5/10 reflects that while cosmetics offer practical visibility value, the seasonal limited-time pricing tactics and aggressive bundling still employ psychological pressure. The FOMO tactics are less problematic due to cosmetic visibility and the rotational availability of past cosmetics.</p><p>Players should balance cosmetic purchases with actual driving time; cosmetics that match your main vehicle provide excellent personal value without requiring purchases for mechanical progression.</p>`
