    history_html: `<p><strong>2012:</strong> Candy Crush Saga launched with a deceptively simple cosmetic shop framework while implementing aggressive monetization through lives systems and power-ups. The game appeared cosmetic-focused while deriving primary revenue from progression monetization.</p><p><strong>2012-2015:</strong> Candy Crush's monetization became increasingly sophisticated, incorporating strategic difficulty spikes and power-up bundling to maximize spending. Lives expiration timers and progression roadblocks forced cosmetic purchases disguised as necessary gameplay investments.</p><p><strong>2015-2020:</strong> The game generated enormous revenue—billions of dollars—through psychological monetization tactics. Algorithmic difficulty balancing increased spending pressure on approaching cosmetic progression limits.</p><p><strong>2020-2025:</strong> Continued refinement of behavioral monetization systems. Lives mechanics, booster bundling, and progression locks remain primary revenue drivers disguised as cosmetic purchases.</p>`,
    controversy_html: `<p><strong>Candy Crush Saga represents one of gaming's most controversial monetization systems.</strong> Criticism focuses on predatory psychological design: artificial difficulty spikes engineered to force cosmetic purchases, lives mechanics creating artificial time pressure, and progression roadblocks blocking advancement without spending. The game has been criticized by consumer advocates and researchers as a manipulation system rather than a game.</p>`,
