    editorial_html: `<p><strong>Candy Crush Saga deserves its fairness score of 1/10 as one of gaming's most predatory monetization systems.</strong> While technically featuring cosmetics, the game's actual monetization centers on behavioral psychology designed to manipulate spending through artificial obstacles and time pressure. This represents not fair cosmetic monetization but sophisticated manipulation disguised as cosmetics.</p><p>King's model combines lives systems that create artificial time pressure, algorithmic difficulty balancing that forces cosmetic purchases, and progression roadblocks that block advancement without spending. The game has been studied by researchers documenting how deliberately the psychological pressure systems are designed to maximize revenue extraction.</p><p>Players should avoid Candy Crush entirely if they struggle with spending discipline. The game is engineered by professional psychologists to override normal spending controls. This isn't a game with unfair cosmetics—it's a monetization system that uses game mechanics as psychological manipulation delivery vehicles. It represents the absolute bottom tier of player respect in gaming.</p>`
